>
  <p class="crumbs"><a href={research}>← Research</a></p>
  <h1>Governed Agentic Empirical Research</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    A chapter proposal for the Springer edited volume <em>Using Artificial
    Intelligence in Empirical Software Engineering Research</em> (eds. Mäntylä,
    Mendez, Franch, Kalinowski). Proposal due 23 October 2026; chapter, if
    accepted, through June 2027.
  </p>

  <h2>Abstract <span class="badge">proposal draft v3 · 7 October 2026</span></h2>
  <div class="abstract">
    <p>
      AI is no longer a tool that a researcher calls at one step of a study. In
      empirical software engineering it now plans, retrieves, extracts,
      critiques, and transforms data, with decisions delegated to it along the
      way. Current guidance asks researchers to disclose the models and prompts
      they used, to keep session traces, and to validate AI output by hand;
      stage-specific frameworks now govern single steps such as literature
      screening or repository mining. What no standard yet specifies is the
      research process itself as a governed object: which decisions were
      delegated to AI and under what authority, which intermediate artifacts
      they produced, and which human checks accepted them, recorded as
      reviewable provenance across the whole study. We already know how to keep
      an engineering process accountable; this chapter
      argues that the research process needs the same treatment once agents are
      participants in it. We present Governed Agentic Empirical Research (GAER),
      a methodological framework in which every agent works under bounded
      authority with an explicit role and rules, every consequential decision
      leaves durable provenance in a shared memory bank, and reproducibility is
      built into the research repository as executable checks rather than
      written up afterwards. The framework is grounded in working tooling and
      illustrated by a time-boxed empirical study in which agents drafted every
      analysis while typed claims, pre-registered thresholds, a human
      re-derivation ledger, and an adversarial reviewer gated what could be
      reported. A PhD student or researcher can adopt it with a short setup and
      no additional infrastructure.
    </p>
  </div>
  <p class="note">
    This is a working draft ahead of submission, published so that
    collaborators can read it in context. Wording, the framework's name and
    the worked example may all change before 23 October.
  </p>

  <h2>Research tracks</h2>
  <div class="cards">
    <a class="card" href={`${base}research/governed-agentic-research/ai-in-empirical-se/`}>
      <span class="card-title">AI and agents across the empirical-SE lifecycle</span>
      <span class="card-desc">What has been published on LLMs and agents at each stage of empirical SE research — study design, literature reviews, repository mining, qualitative and quantitative analysis, artifact generation, reporting — and what it says about rigor, validity, reproducibility and the human role. Anchored on Gomes et al. (2026).</span>
      <span class="card-meta">{lifecycle.length} sources · 6 October 2026 · synthesis, source explorer, Consensus searches, literature-gap table</span>
    </a>
  </div>

  <h2>How this relates to the other projects</h2>
  <p>
    The chapter is the methodological layer on top of work that is reported
    elsewhere on this site. Each project below supplies one strand of the
    argument; none of them is the chapter.
  </p>
  <ul class="relations">
    <li>
      <a href={`${soa}agentic-harnesses/`}>Agentic harnesses</a> and
      <a href={`${soa}agentic-memory/`}>Agent memory</a> (the
      <a href={soa}>State of the Art in Agentic SE</a> project) are themselves
      governed research passes: schema-constrained source records, discovery
      separated from an adversarially prompted verification pass, provenance
      fields on every record. They are the method's literature-review stage in
      practice — one stage of the lifecycle, not the whole process.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Research.AI</strong> (the <code>agentic-kg</code> project under
      the knowledge-graphs tenet) supplies the chapter's sharpest case for human
      methodological judgement: reconciling a gold-label schema where the
      labelling choice itself could distort measured precision and recall, so
      the evaluation would measure the researchers' decision rather than the
      system's behaviour.
    </li>
    <li>
      A time-boxed empirical study run in August 2026 under the framework's
      tooling (<code>agentic-governance</code>, <code>agentic-research</code>)
      is the worked example in the abstract: typed claims, pre-registered
      thresholds, a human re-derivation ledger and an adversarial reviewer
      gating what could be reported. Changes that came out of it flowed back
      into both tools.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Status</h2>
  <p>
    Repository private until the chapter is accepted. The proposal's evidence
    phase (internal evidence map, literature-gap review, failure-mode
    inventory) is under way; three competing pitches and an adversarial review
    precede the final one-page proposal.
  </p>
